<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Baden-Powell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Robert Stephenson Smyth Baden-Powell, Lord Baden-Powell, Stephe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Baden-Powell, 1st Baron Baden-Powell, was a British Army officer and writer who founded The Boy Scouts Association and, with his sister Agnes, The Girl Guides Association. He served in the army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from 1876 until 1910, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> in 1899 he defended the town in the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahikeng">Mafeking</a>. In August 1907 he held an experimental camp on Brownsea Island to test his ideas for training boys, and in 1908 he published Scouting for Boys, which helped the rapid growth of the Scout Movement. He retired from guiding the two associations in 1937 and died in Nyeri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, in 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-baden-powell-1st-baron-baden-powell.jpg" alt="Robert Baden-Powell" width="330" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Russell &amp; Sons [ 1 ]; derivative work by Robertus Pius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Baden-Powell,_Bain_news_service_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baden-Powell was born Robert Stephenson Smyth Powell in Paddington, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 22 February 1857, the second youngest son of Baden Powell, Savilian Professor of Geometry at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> priest. His father died in 1860, and his mother then styled the family name Baden-Powell to tie her children to her late husband's fame. The name was legally changed by Royal Licence on 30 April 1902. His godfather was the railway engineer Robert Stephenson, and the family called him Stephe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Commissioned in the 13th Hussars in 1876, he drew maps of the Battle of Maiwand in 1880 and sharpened his scouting skills among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> in Natal. In 1896 he served in the Second Matabele War, commanding reconnaissance missions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matopos-hills">Matopos Hills</a>, where he met the American scout Frederick Russell Burnham, who introduced him to woodcraft and the campaign hat.</p><p>In July 1899 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahikeng">Mafeking</a> to gather intelligence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> and built up stores and a garrison there. The ensuing siege lasted 217 days, the town being surrounded at times by more than 8,000 Boers. The garrison used deceptions, including fake minefields and soldiers pretending to avoid non-existent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbed-wire">barbed wire</a>. He was later Inspector-General of Cavalry from 1903 and was promoted lieutenant-general in 1907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His military manual Aids to Scouting (1899) became a best-seller after his return in 1903 and was used by teachers and youth organisations. Encouraged by Sir William Alexander Smith of the Boys' Brigade, he rewrote it for young readers and in August 1907 held a camp on Brownsea Island with about twenty boys to test his ideas. He had also been influenced by Ernest Thompson Seton, the founder of the Woodcraft Indians.</p><p>Scouting for Boys appeared in six instalments in 1908 and has sold approximately 150 million copies. He married Olave St Clair Soames on 30 October 1912, and in 1937 he was appointed to the Order of Merit. He spent most of his last two years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and his grave in Nyeri is a national monument.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Baden-Powell, 1st Baron Baden-Powell” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first instalment of <b>Robert Baden-Powell</b>'s handbook on outdoor skills and citizenship appeared in London.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
